[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October. That settles two of them; the third
is still open.

<Callout kind="warn">

#4471 is closed, and the one-day problem is not fixed. Its reports have their own open ticket:
[#3](https://github.com/karolszymonczyk/mega-support-kb/issues/3).

</Callout>

## Where each problem stands

- **`parser` — fixed.** Engineering shipped `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
  [#1](https://github.com/karolszymonczyk/mega-support-kb/issues/1) is closed. To check: two tenants
  write `en-GB` and `en-IE`. If the fix reads a list of EU locales, not the locale's own date order,
  `en-GB` may still swap.
- **`hint` — won't fix.** The booking-v3 form is retired, so its `MM/DD/YYYY` hint reaches nobody
  now. [#2](https://github.com/karolszymonczyk/mega-support-kb/issues/2) is closed as not planned.
- **`one-day` — open.** Neither change touches a date that shifts by a day across timezones.
  [#3](https://github.com/karolszymonczyk/mega-support-kb/issues/3) stays open until someone owns it.

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

**`parser` — day and month swap.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant
writes the day first, so a date with a day of 12 or lower comes out swapped: "Booked for
2 December. The invite says 12 February." App and email reports, and form reports from
booking-v2 tenants, whose hint already reads DD/MM/YYYY.

**`hint` — the booking-v3 form asks for the month first.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under
its date field, and its tenants write the day first. People type what the hint says; the form reads
it the tenant's way. Only form reports from booking-v3 tenants. Fixing the parser does not touch
this one.

**`one-day` — the date moves by a day.** Not a swap: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday." A timezone or day-boundary conversion, not the parser. The desk already noted
these as one-day moves, then filed them under `parser` by tenant.

## How these were counted

- One report per id: the export pages overlap, so some rows come twice.
- Cause by tenant, never by intake's tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser; the tenant's
  locale and booking form decide (`causes.ts`). Intake's tag disagrees with the cause for many of
  these reports.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) counts there, not under `parser`.

## Lessons

- Merging by subject line is fast, and it hides causes. Before you mark a big ticket known, split
  it by cause and check that each part has a ticket.
- A footnote under a table is not a ticket.
- Closing the big ticket is not the same as fixing what was merged into it. Check each part.

